\begin{proof}
    Set $m_{n+1} := \sum_{i = 1}^nm_i$ and $d := m_{n+1} + 1$. The following observations are immediate.
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $m_i \leq d -1$ for $i\in\{1,\cdots, n+1\}$.
        \item $\sum_{i = 1}^{n+1}m_i = 2d-2$.
        \item $n+1\leq \sum_{i = 1}^nm_i + 1 = d$.
    \end{itemize}
    By Theorem~\ref{kevin}, there exist a critically fixed rational map $P$ such that  $\text{Crit}(P)=\{c_1,\cdots,c_{n+1}\}$ and the multiplicity of the critical point $c_i$ is $m_i$, $i\in\{1,\cdots,n+1\}$. As the critical point $c_{n+1}$ of the degree $d$ rational map $P$ has multiplicity $m_{n+1}=d-1$ (i.e., $c_{n+1}$ is a fully ramified critical point), the map $P$ can be chosen to be a polynomial, up to M{\"o}bius conjugation.
\end{proof}

